1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,145
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,145 --> 00:00:08,850
In this video we review the report, Building an LLM Evaluation Framework: Architecture, Statistics, and Practice.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:14,101
It's a design guide for teams who want eval numbers they can actually act on.

4
00:00:14,101 --> 00:00:26,306
We'll walk through the architecture it proposes, the statistics it argues should be built in, how it treats LLM judges, and then give our own assessment of its strengths and gaps.

5
00:00:27,806 --> 00:00:30,649
The paper starts from a simple observation.

6
00:00:30,649 --> 00:00:35,013
Classical machine learning had a held-out test set and one metric.

7
00:00:35,013 --> 00:00:36,996
LLMs break that in three ways.

8
00:00:36,996 --> 00:00:41,426
Outputs are open-ended, so correctness is often not a string match.

9
00:00:41,426 --> 00:00:44,136
The same model serves thousands of tasks.

10
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:47,905
And what we ship is usually a pipeline, not a bare model.

11
00:00:47,905 --> 00:00:57,161
Meanwhile, most internal evals are ad-hoc scripts with no error bars, graders nobody validated, and data that may have leaked into training.

12
00:00:58,661 --> 00:01:02,885
From those failure modes, the authors derive seven requirements.

13
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:06,515
Results must be reproducible, and statistically honest.

14
00:01:06,515 --> 00:01:12,852
The framework should be composable, and any model-based grader must be validated against humans.

15
00:01:12,852 --> 00:01:17,340
Results need to be sliceable, so an average can't hide a regression.

16
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,377
The system under test can be a whole pipeline.

17
00:01:20,377 --> 00:01:23,941
And evals must be cheap enough to run on every change.

18
00:01:25,441 --> 00:01:29,513
Here is the core of the paper: a seven-part architecture.

19
00:01:29,513 --> 00:01:37,799
In blue is what gets evaluated: a declarative task spec and a versioned dataset with slice metadata and cluster IDs.

20
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:48,729
In orange is how it runs: a model adapter that can wrap a model, a RAG system, or an agent, and a runner with concurrency, retries, and a response cache.

21
00:01:48,729 --> 00:01:55,158
In green is how it's judged: scorers, a statistical aggregator, and reports with CI gates.

22
00:01:55,158 --> 00:01:59,158
Underneath everything is a content-addressed run record.

23
00:01:59,158 --> 00:02:07,873
Hash the task, data, prompt, model, parameters, scorer, and seed, and two runs with the same hash are the same experiment.

24
00:02:07,873 --> 00:02:12,731
That single design choice is what makes every past number auditable.

25
00:02:14,231 --> 00:02:17,380
For scoring, the paper ranks four families.

26
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:23,385
Programmatic checks come first: exact match, regex, schema validation, unit tests.

27
00:02:23,385 --> 00:02:25,728
They're deterministic and cheap.

28
00:02:25,728 --> 00:02:31,587
Reference similarity metrics like BLEU and ROUGE are useful mainly as tripwires.

29
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:38,178
LLM judges are flexible, but the paper insists they're instruments that must be validated.

30
00:02:38,178 --> 00:02:43,230
And human review is the ground truth that calibrates everything else.

31
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:46,746
The guiding principle is: decompose, then check.

32
00:02:46,746 --> 00:02:52,091
Break a fuzzy notion of quality into criteria you can check individually.

33
00:02:53,592 --> 00:02:57,682
The statistics section is where the paper is most valuable.

34
00:02:57,682 --> 00:03:01,010
Every score is an estimate from a finite sample.

35
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:10,786
On the left: at seventy percent accuracy, a one-hundred-item eval has a ninety-five percent confidence interval of plus or minus nine points.

36
00:03:10,786 --> 00:03:14,322
You need about a thousand items to get under three.

37
00:03:14,322 --> 00:03:17,858
On the right is the fix for comparisons: pair them.

38
00:03:17,858 --> 00:03:22,711
Score both systems on the same items and analyze per-item differences.

39
00:03:22,711 --> 00:03:32,695
At a thousand items, the smallest detectable difference drops from five point seven points to four point one when the correlation is point five.

40
00:03:32,695 --> 00:03:38,727
That's the same as halving the sample size you need, and it costs nothing to implement.

41
00:03:40,227 --> 00:03:42,183
Two more statistical points.

42
00:03:42,183 --> 00:03:47,212
First, items are often clustered, like ten questions about one document.

43
00:03:47,212 --> 00:03:57,481
With ten items per cluster and a modest intra-cluster correlation of point two, the real standard error is one point six seven times the naive one.

44
00:03:57,481 --> 00:04:03,000
So your ninety-five percent interval actually covers about seventy-six percent.

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,030
The fix is just to store a cluster ID and compute cluster-robust errors.

46
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:11,872
Second, do power analysis before you build the dataset.

47
00:04:11,872 --> 00:04:19,346
Start from the smallest difference that would change your decision, and derive the number of items from it.

48
00:04:20,846 --> 00:04:24,139
The most memorable result concerns LLM judges.

49
00:04:24,139 --> 00:04:36,453
If a judge agrees with ground truth with probability a, and errors are symmetric, the measured difference between two systems is two a minus one, times the true difference.

50
00:04:36,453 --> 00:04:39,818
The paper checks this with a seeded simulation.

51
00:04:39,818 --> 00:04:50,485
A judge with eighty percent agreement turns a real five-point improvement into about three points, and across replicate runs the range includes zero.

52
00:04:50,485 --> 00:04:55,639
So the improvement is no longer reliably detectable at a thousand items.

53
00:04:55,639 --> 00:04:59,219
Judge quality trades directly against sample size.

54
00:05:00,719 --> 00:05:03,726
So the paper gives a calibration protocol.

55
00:05:03,726 --> 00:05:07,164
Build a gold set of a few hundred expert labels.

56
00:05:07,164 --> 00:05:17,261
Measure agreement with accuracy, Cohen's kappa, and a confusion matrix, which tells you whether errors are symmetric noise or one-sided bias.

57
00:05:17,261 --> 00:05:24,637
Test for position bias by swapping answer order, and for verbosity bias by regressing scores on length.

58
00:05:24,637 --> 00:05:33,588
Mitigate with both orderings, simple binary rubrics, reasoning before the verdict, and a judge from a different model family.

59
00:05:33,588 --> 00:05:36,596
And re-validate whenever anything changes.

60
00:05:38,096 --> 00:05:40,751
The paper then covers data integrity.

61
00:05:40,751 --> 00:05:51,730
Public benchmarks may be in training data, so keep private held-out sets, embed canary strings, use statistical contamination tests, and add fresh items.

62
00:05:51,730 --> 00:05:59,839
For RAG, it recommends scoring retrieval, grounding, and answer quality separately, so you can localize failures.

63
00:05:59,839 --> 00:06:12,756
For agents: sandboxed, resettable environments, scoring the final state rather than the transcript, repeated trials reported as both pass at k and pass hat k, and cost per success.

64
00:06:14,256 --> 00:06:24,408
Operationally, the paper recommends three tiers: a smoke tier on every commit, a regression tier on every merge, and a full tier with human review before release.

65
00:06:24,408 --> 00:06:27,855
Gates should be statistical tests, not raw comparisons.

66
00:06:27,855 --> 00:06:32,743
A rule like 'the score must not drop' fires on pure noise about half the time.

67
00:06:32,743 --> 00:06:39,072
Instead, fail only if the lower confidence bound of the paired difference falls below your threshold.

68
00:06:39,072 --> 00:06:43,333
Close the loop with production traffic, and always read transcripts.

69
00:06:44,834 --> 00:06:46,124
Now our assessment.

70
00:06:46,124 --> 00:06:54,414
The main strength is that the paper treats evaluation as measurement, and backs that up with numbers anyone can reproduce.

71
00:06:54,414 --> 00:07:02,499
The architecture's separation of concerns is clean, and the content-addressed run record is a genuinely practical idea.

72
00:07:02,499 --> 00:07:06,033
The task spec and checklist can be adopted directly.

73
00:07:06,033 --> 00:07:14,798
On the other side, there are no experiments with real models or real judges, so the calibration protocol is untested in practice.

74
00:07:14,798 --> 00:07:20,097
The judge analysis assumes symmetric noise, while real judges are also biased.

75
00:07:20,097 --> 00:07:23,562
The power formulas are large-sample approximations.

76
00:07:23,562 --> 00:07:27,639
And the architecture is a design, not yet an implementation.

77
00:07:27,639 --> 00:07:33,958
A reference implementation, with a case study on a real task, would be the natural next step.

78
00:07:35,458 --> 00:07:37,239
To wrap up, five takeaways.

79
00:07:37,239 --> 00:07:41,526
A hundred-item eval can't see differences under about ten points.

80
00:07:41,526 --> 00:07:44,494
Compare systems with paired tests by default.

81
00:07:44,494 --> 00:07:47,660
Store cluster IDs and use cluster-robust errors.

82
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:53,398
Calibrate your judges, because an eighty percent judge can hide a real five-point gain.

83
00:07:53,398 --> 00:07:58,015
And hash everything into a run record so every number is reproducible.

84
00:07:58,015 --> 00:08:03,622
The paper, its figures, and the scripts that generate them are all in the repository.

85
00:08:03,622 --> 00:08:04,941
Thanks for watching.
